### Phase 1 — Core physics, controls, anti-clipping (fixes B4, B7, B9, B8-input)
1. **Physics engine:** use **Rapier (WASM)** (add `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat` if absent; keep existing if already Rapier). **Fixed timestep 1/120 s** with accumulator and render interpolation; gravity `(0, −3.73, 0)` from constants; CCD on for fast bodies; sleeping enabled.
2. **Ground = one source of truth.** Terrain is generated from a height array/function; the **same data** builds both the render mesh and the Rapier **heightfield collider** so feet never sink or float. Provide `groundHeightAt(x, z)` that samples that same data.
3. **Structures:** each habitat module gets compound colliders (cuboid/cylinder/convex), **floors ≥ 0.3 m thick, walls ≥ 0.2 m**, no thin planes, so nothing tunnels. Rocks ≥ 0.3 m get static colliders streamed within ~30 m of the player; smaller rocks are visual only (the player steps over them).
4. **Player:** Rapier `KinematicCharacterController` with capsule (radius 0.35 m, height 1.8 m), `offset ≈ 0.02`, **autostep ≤ 0.25 m**, `snapToGround ≈ 0.2 m`, max climb slope ≈ 30° (regolith friction angle limit), slide above ~35°. Vertical velocity integrates gravity 3.73 m/s². **Jump** sets takeoff speed from the suited jump above (2.10 m/s). Show fall, hang time and landing impact (a soft landing thud, no damage under 3 m).
5. **Movement speeds** from the Froude values above (walk 0.86 m/s, transition 1.1 m/s, lope up to ~3 m/s [SIM]); acceleration is gradual (suited mass 160 kg [SIM]).
6. **Mouse look:** a single click on the canvas calls `requestPointerLock()` (use `unadjustedMovement: true` where supported); mouse movement rotates the camera with `movementX/Y`, pitch clamped ±89°; **no click-drag needed**. **Esc** releases the cursor and opens the **pause menu** (Resume, Settings, Quit to menu); handle `pointerlockerror` and window blur by pausing. Keys: `W A S D` move, `Space` jump, `Shift` lope, `E` interact. Sensitivity setting included.
7. **Tests (Vitest):** free-fall time and impact speed from 2 m; jump apex and hang time (suited and unsuited); walking-speed constants; wind force ratio. **E2E (Playwright):** teleport the player to ten random points and walk into walls and rocks for 10 simulated minutes; assert the capsule bottom stays within 5 cm of `groundHeightAt` and never enters any structure collider; drop 100 test bodies on the terrain and assert none ends below the surface.

### Phase 2 — Terrain, rocks, hills from NASA statistics (fixes B12 part 1)
1. **Terrain:** chunked heightfield (e.g. 64 × 64 m chunks) from layered noise (broad undulation + ridged hills + simple craters with depth/diameter ≈ 0.2 for fresh craters, shallower for degraded `[VERIFY]`), plus dunes/ripples. Slopes: keep most walkable ground under ~20°; a few steeper features. Optional: if you can obtain a real HiRISE/MOLA DEM crop quickly and license-safely, use it and log it; otherwise procedural, **tuned to the statistics here**.
2. **Rocks with the Golombek exponential model:** for a patch with rock fraction `k` (default 0.05 plains; 0.02 sandy hollows; up to 0.19 rocky patches), draw diameters from `n(D) dD = k·q·exp(−qD) / (π·D²/4) dD`, `q = 1.79 + 0.152/k`, minimum D = 0.1 m for instanced meshes (smaller pebbles by shader detail). Rock height ≈ 0.3–0.6 × D, partly buried, random orientation, slightly angular shapes (displaced icosahedrons), dust-coated on top. Use InstancedMesh; log `k` per patch in the HUD "Mars Lens".
3. **Regolith behaviour:** surface friction μ = tan(33°) ≈ 0.65 for boots and wheels; add 1–2 **soft-sand patches** where rover wheel sinkage reaches ~50% of wheel diameter and traction drops (a physical reason the rover can get stuck; the game rule from the design doc still decides story outcomes).
4. **Footprints:** pooled decals, fading with wind over time; **ballistic dust**: kicked particles follow `y'' = −3.73` with negligible air drag (they hang longer and arc higher than on Earth).

### Phase 3 — Crew, astronaut model, rover (fixes B1, B2, B5, B6, B8)
**Astronaut (all crew + first-person arms):**
- Redesign the suit from real EVA conventions, **without logos or flags**: hard white upper torso, **gold reflective sun visor** (environment-map reflection; no face visible, as in real EVA, which also avoids the uncanny face), helmet with side lights, **visible metal neck ring and soft neck seal** (head overlaps the neck so no gap), chest control box, **life-support backpack** with antenna, articulated shoulders/elbows/knees with bearing rings, gloves with coloured wrist rings, lugged boots, belt with tool loops. Procedural suit-fabric normal map, dust accumulation on lower legs and boots (rust-orange gradient).
- Each of the 4 crew has a different **stripe colour** and a text-only name tag. **Never render a realistic human face.** If an indoor scene needs helmets off, use a clean stylised head, or keep helmets on.
- **First-person view-model:** two gloved arms and hands rendered in a **separate pass/near-plane layer** so they never clip into walls; procedurally animated by physics: inertia spring sway, walking bob scaled by gait and low gravity (slower, floatier), arms swing in the lope, raise-to-interact gesture on `E`, breathing sway. Optional tool in hand (tablet or sample tool).
- **Procedural gait:** no animation clips for locomotion. Stride length and frequency derive from speed and g (Froude scaling: longer ground contact and lower cadence than Earth); duty factor falls with speed; flight phase when above the transition speed; **two-bone leg IK** plants each foot on the terrain slope using ground raycasts; pelvis height adapts; body leans into acceleration and slopes.

**Crew NPCs (the four crew defined in the design doc §2):**
- Place them in the world. Each runs the **same character controller, gravity, speeds and gait** as the player.
- **Autonomy:** a finite-state machine (Idle, Patrol, Work at station, Walk to task, EVA, Rest, Alarm) with navigation on a waypoint graph (indoors) and a coarse grid/navmesh outdoors (use `three-pathfinding` or `recast-navigation` if already available, else a simple A*). They walk between modules, check solar panels, tend the greenhouse, work at consoles, sit/rest.
- **Reactions to game state:** oxygen leak → crew move to the valve and the airlock; dust storm → everyone returns inside; low Power → lights dim and crew walk slower; low Health → limp (shorter stride) and sit down; high Stress → pacing; a decision's effect is shown with a clear visual cue plus the crew panel. Looking at a crew member and pressing `E` shows their status as **text only** (no voice).
- Crew never push the player or the rover.

**Rover (rebuild; design inspired by the Perseverance-class rover, no NASA/JPL logos, no flag decal, no plates):**
- **Model:** body (warm electronics box) with equipment deck; **six 0.525 m wheels** (aluminium look, 48 grousers, curved spokes); **rocker-bogie** (left and right rockers, bogies, a differential bar); mast with camera head at ~2.2 m; folded robotic arm with turret at the front; rear radioisotope-style power unit with cooling fins (generic); high-gain antenna on the deck; hazard cameras. Mass 1,025 kg.
- **Physics:** **6-wheel raycast suspension** (Rapier `DynamicRayCastVehicleController` or equivalent) with per-wheel suspension travel, plus a chassis collider. Visually drive the rocker/bogie arms and differential from wheel heights (body pitch = average of the two rocker angles, as in the real mechanism) so the rover **tilts naturally over slopes and rocks**. Wheels never enter terrain or objects (wheel radius + suspension clamp + chassis collider). Front and rear wheels steer (arcing turns and point turns). Wheel rotation = ground speed ÷ wheel radius (1.65 m of travel per revolution). Tilt warning at 30°, tip limit 45° (NASA design limit).
- **Behaviour (fixes the "moves at a light touch" bug):** a state machine with hysteresis. `PARKED` (parking brake on, body sleeping, high static friction) → if the player is farther than **8 m**, `FOLLOWING` (path toward the player, avoiding rocks and steep slopes) → at **4 m** return to `PARKED`. Inside the 4–8 m band it never starts moving. The player or crew bumping it must not move it noticeably (mass 1,025 kg, brake on). Speed: show the real top speed (0.042 m/s) in the Mars Lens, and use a clearly labelled **simulation drive speed** (e.g. 1.0 m/s) for gameplay.
- **Wheel dust:** ballistic particles behind the wheels, small tread marks that fade.

### Phase 4 — Martian sky, light and atmosphere (fixes B11)
1. **Sky shader (no Earth Rayleigh blue):** dust-dominated scattering. Day sky is **butterscotch**: darker tan-brown toward the zenith, paler toward the horizon. Add a **Mie-like forward-scattering lobe** (Henyey–Greenstein, g ≈ 0.7 [SIM]) that favours blue near the Sun, producing the **blue halo (up to ~25–30°) that grows near sunrise/sunset**, while the sky away from the Sun turns rose/butterscotch. **Twilight lasts long** (sky stays lit until the Sun is well below the horizon).
2. **Sun disc:** angular diameter ≈ 21 arcmin (about two-thirds of the Sun seen from Earth); brightness 43% of Earth's (`586.2/1361`); slightly blue-white near sunset.
3. **Dust optical depth `tau` drives everything** (design doc: tau 1.0 → power 1.00; tau 2.1 → 0.74; tau > 3 → 0.28): direct beam `exp(−tau / max(cosZenith, 0.05))`, diffuse fraction rises as direct falls, so at high tau the Sun disc vanishes, shadows soften to almost nothing, the sky turns brown and visibility drops (fog density from tau). Light intensity follows the design-doc power curve.
4. **Night sky:** stars visible, dimmed near the horizon by dust, Milky Way band; **Phobos** (one-third the width of our Moon, fast: 7.65 h orbit, rising in the west and setting in the east) and **Deimos** (a bright star-like point, 30.3 h); Earth as a bright bluish star near the Sun in morning/evening sky `[VERIFY]`.
5. **Lighting rig:** one directional Sun with a single good shadow map + strong hemisphere/sky fill (dusty sky gives a lot of diffuse light) + environment map from the sky; ACES filmic tone mapping; exposure set for 586 W/m².
6. **Day cycle:** based on the 24 h 39 m sol, time-compressed for gameplay (label "simulation time").
7. **Dust devils (visual only)** occasionally cross the plain.

